-f886af15f9ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106748 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106749 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106749 #edb/ref 1061 2652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106749 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e10f3c1-5b3b-5adb-9fd0-0a1e4d8f3f23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106749 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106750 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106750 #edb/ref 1061 2654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106750 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bcefc791-a469-5133-b984-bf5f1294532f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106750 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106751 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106751 #edb/ref 1061 2656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106751 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ade971db-c5ab-5cf8-bede-d4db99d2b5f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106751 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106752 #edb/ref 1061 2660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c44388fb-c586-558c-b35f-1e1ef27bb6fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107031 #edb/ref 1064 "Rational functions have infinite limits when the $x$-value approaches vertical asymptotes. To find the infinite limits of a rational function $f(x)$, we first find the vertical asymptotes.\n\nFor example, consider the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-2}\n$$\n\nThis rational function has a vertical asymptote at $x=2$.\n\nTo find the one-sided limits at the asymptote $x=2$, we check the sign of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and the right.\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^-$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $1.9$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-10$ | $-100$ | $-1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are negative, and they get more and more negative. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^+$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the right:\n\n| $x$ | $2.1$ | $2.01$ | $2.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $10$ | $100$ | $1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are positive, and they get larger and larger. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nThese one-sided limits match up with what we see in the graph of $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/24/248e651214f9339d5e47f09f3eecd3387897eda525d99845258d4466025f16ac.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107031 #edb/ref 1065 1338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107031 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c5a2dd1f-90e1-5bcc-a854-1debabaffe1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107031 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
